// calendar-demo.script — a comprehensive showcase of the calendar package.
//
// The calendar package draws month-grid calendars over the \picture layer. This demo
// exercises every feature of \calendar: the default Sunday-start month, the Monday-start
// (ISO) variant, the leap-year February, the today highlight, and the configuration
// variables that restyle the box — colours, cell size and type sizes. We frame it as a
// short article over the document format.

\use{document}
\use{calendar}

\def headline {\italic{The calendar package} \hfil page \the\pageno}
\def footline {\hfil — \the\pageno — \hfil}

\title{The calendar Package}
\author{Month grids drawn in the document language}
\date{}
\maketitle

A \italic{calendar} is one \mono{\\picture} box: a title bar with the month and year, a
weekday header row, and a grid of day cells. It flows in the text like any other box, so
it can be centred on a page, dropped into a paragraph, or tiled into a year planner. The
whole package is the document language — \mono{\\calc} does the date arithmetic, a pair
of maps hold the names and month lengths, and the grid is \mono{\\picture} lines and
placed labels. There is no calendar-specific engine primitive.

\section{A month}

The call \mono{\\calendar\{2026\}\{6\}} draws June 2026. Weeks start on Sunday and the
weekend columns take a distinct ink.

\vskip 10pt
\centerline{\calendar{2026}{6}}

\section{Weeks starting on Monday}

Set \mono{\\calweekstart} to \mono{1} for the ISO convention: the header rotates so
Monday leads and the weekend pair moves to the right of the grid. The change is scoped to
the group, so it affects only this calendar.

\vskip 10pt
{\set calweekstart {1}
\centerline{\calendar{2026}{6}}}

\section{Leap years and the today highlight}

February is twenty-nine days long exactly when the Gregorian rule says so — 2024 is a leap
year. Setting \mono{\\caltoday} draws a tinted cell behind that day; here the fourth is
marked.

\vskip 10pt
{\set caltoday {4}\set calweekstart {1}
\centerline{\calendar{2024}{2}}}

\eject

\section{Restyling}

Every visual property is a configuration variable set with \mono{\\set} after
\mono{\\use}. Below, a warmer palette, larger cells and a Monday start turn the same
primitive into a wall-calendar page.

\vskip 10pt
{\set calcw {54}\set calch {46}
 \set caltitleh {30}\set calheadh {22}
 \set caltitlesize {17}\set calheadsize {11}\set caldaysize {14}
 \set calweekstart {1}
 \set caltitlebg {#9c4221}\set calheadbg {#fffaf0}\set calheadink {#7b341e}
 \set calgrid {#dd9b6c}\set calweekendink {#b7791f}\set caltodayfill {#feebc8}
 \set caltoday {25}
 \centerline{\calendar{2026}{12}}}

\section{A quarter, side by side}

Because a calendar is just a box, a row of \mono{\\calendar} calls in an \mono{\\hbox}
sets three months together — the start of a year planner.

\vskip 10pt
{\set calcw {26}\set calch {22}
 \set caltitleh {20}\set calheadh {14}
 \set caltitlesize {11}\set calheadsize {7}\set caldaysize {8}
 \centerline{\hbox{\calendar{2026}{1}\hskip 12pt\calendar{2026}{2}\hskip 12pt\calendar{2026}{3}}}}

Every calendar on these pages is one \mono{\\picture} box, set by the same engine as the
prose around it.
